  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 PM EDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nIt will feel more like mid-summer than mid-October over the next \ncouple of days thanks to upper ridging building a little further \nnorth and sfc high pressure slowly retreating off to the southeast. \nThis will allow for strong south to southwesterly flow to continue \nto advect in warm air from the south as well as increase low-level \nmoisture from the Gulf. Highs will top out into the in the low 90s \nto upper 80s tomorrow and could be close on Wednesday but increasing \ncloud cover ahead of the approaching system will likely keep \ntemperatures in the mid to upper 80s. \n\nAs a sfc boundary approaches from the west and then slows over the \nOhio Valley for the middle of the week, a surge of Gulf moisture \nwill pool along the boundary increasing dew points into the \nmid/upper 60s tomorrow and into the low 70s on Wednesday. There \nseems to be a fair amount of model agreement that we could also see \nPWAT values over 2.00\". While we will see a good surge of moisture \nand warmth ahead of the approaching boundary, current forecast has \nonly about a quarter to around three-quarters of an inch with the \nhighest amounts along the Ohio River into southern IN. LREF Ensemble \ncurrently has a 20 percent probability of getting an over an inch \nwhile NBM is around 40 percent. \n\nAs the boundary approaches Tuesday night into Wednesday we will see \nthe potential for showers and thunderstorms to develop. While we \nshould have a fair amount of instability thanks to the warm and \nmoist conditions ahead of the boundary, the best dynamics, lift and \nshear remain further to our north into central and northern IN and \ninto Ohio. Some of the model profiles do suggest that if we get a \nthunderstorm an isolated strong gusty wind and heavy rainfall would \nbe the main impacts. As of now, SPC Day 3 convective outlook keeps \nour area in general thunder. \n\nBoundary will push through by Friday with cooler and drier weather \nfiltering in behind. Friday looks to be mostly dry with highs \nranging from the mid/upper 60s along and north of the Ohio River to \nthe low/mid 70s across central and southern KY. This will setup a \ndry yet cool weekend as the core of the cool air will be in place on \nSaturday, with highs struggling to get out of the 50s into the 60s \nand we could see lows drop into the 30s with a potential of our \nfirst frost Sunday morning.",
